WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.360
So, what would it actually take to build a
Death Star?

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:07.100
Hey there, Crazies.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.780
You may or may not have seen it before, but
there’s this blog post from 2012

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.720
that asks a really fun question:

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.780
How Much Would It Cost To Build The Death Star?

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.300
They estimated it would cost about 850 quadrillion
U.S. Dollars

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.480
and would take over 830,000 years just to
produce the steel.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.480
Well, that’s boring!

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.520
We can do better.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.620
The blog assumed the Death Star’s density
would be comparable to an aircraft carrier,

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.200
which might make sense if present day humans
were building it,

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.440
but it was built by a Galactic Empire a long
time ago in a galaxy far, far away.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.900
How can it be a long time ago if it’s in
the future?

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.860
It’s not in the future, it’s in the past.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.100
But there are spaceships.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:44.640
Ugh.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.900
Imagine a group of humans from the Star Wars
galaxy want to live off-the-grid.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.340
They venture out into intergalactic space
in search of a new home.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.540
Finally, they find a remote planet with a
thriving ecosystem and settle down.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.420
In an effort to shelter their children from
woes of technology, they don’t pass on the

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.060
stories of where they came from.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.560
Their history is forgotten.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.380
That planet they settled on is Earth hundreds
of thousands of years ago.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.140
Just go with it!

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.940
OK, so back to the aircraft carrier, its hallways
look like this.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.940
Whereas, hallways on the Death Star look like
this.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.120
Way more room!!

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.140
They’re, like, twice as tall and 6 times
as wide.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:21.000
Huge!

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.000
The blog chose to use the HMS Illustrious,
which has a mass of 22,000 metric tons and

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:30.830
a volume of about 28,600 cubic meters.

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.460
That’s over 1000 school buses worth of space.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.700
The Death Star hallways are bigger, so it
should be significantly less dense.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.130
An average of 4 times as much radial space
gives us 64x the volume

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:48.240
or 64 times less dense than the Illustrious:
The first Death Star was 120 km across.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.820
Are you quoting the technical manual again?

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:51.840
That’s not canon.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.500
Star Wars Legends is the only place I can
get my data!

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.880
So 120 km across means it has a radius of
60 km.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.200
Here’s a size chart, just so you can get
your bearings.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.000
That’s a volume of 900 trillion cubic meters.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.580
Multiplying by density, we get a mass of 11
trillion metric tons

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.260
or 11 quadrillion kg
That’s enough to have a noticeable gravitational pull,

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.750
but not nearly enough to collapse and form a solid

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:17.750
steel sphere.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:19.200
Now let’s estimate cost.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.000
It’s really expensive to get things into
space, even with a space elevator,

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.540
so let’s assume the steel was mined and
produced in space from asteroids.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.220
There a lot less gravity and a lot more iron.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:35.500
The average cost of steel on Earth in 2016
is about $400 per metric ton.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.840
The steel needed to make a Death Star
would cost 4.4 quadrillion dollars.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.480
about 57 times the current gross-world-product.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:44.700
But that’s just the Earth.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.440
A galactic empire is going to have a lot of
planet, significantly more than 57,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.720
so I think it’s safe to assume the wealth
exists in the empire.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:53.960
But how long will it all take?

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.660
In the Star Wars universe, they were beginning
construction

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.410
at the end of Revenge of the Sith and finished
just before A New Hope.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.430
That’s about 19 years or so.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:07.720
They’d need about 580 billion metric tons
of steel each year just to finish on time.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.940
Even with production rates like present-day Earth.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.420
it would only take 350 planet-sized mining
operations to produce the steel.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.280
Totally reasonable for a galactic empire,
especially if they produce faster.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.880
Although, it’s getting a little harder to keep this a secret.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.180
Anyway, one last thing to consider: the power
source.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.480
The Death Star is powered by Kyber Crystals.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.580
The same thing they use for the light sabers,
just way bigger.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.740
Yes, but that needs a dose of science.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.920
Turning a planet like the Earth into an asteroid
field would take a lot of energy.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.640
280 million trillion trillion joules to be
exact.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:44.670
That’s 57 billion times the total energy
ever used by human beings

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:48.510
or 8.5 times the total energy output of the
Sun per day

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.140
for one shot.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:53.860
It’s no surprise the Death Star requires
24-hours to recharge.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:55.360
That’s also not canon.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.260
I don’t care!

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:57.960
OK, so back to the Kyber Crystals.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.920
Whether they’re allowing for a stable anti-matter
reaction,

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:04.450
or collecting Hawking radiation from evaporating
micro-black holes,

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.780
or siphoning matter off the surface of a very
large star like in Force Awakens.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:14.960
That fuel will have a minimum mass of 3.1
quadrillion kg because E equals mc squared.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.380
That’s a little under 1/3 of the mass of
the steel needed to make the Death Star.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.120
I think it’s safe to assume the crystals
themselves don’t have that much mass.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.820
The Death Star was already hard enough to
move around as it was.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:27.460
We don’t need to be making it any more massive.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:30.300
We also know they’re not firing this thing
from a different star system.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.580
It could take anywhere from 5 to 
100,000 years to reach its target.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.740
So they must be collecting this mass-energy
after they get to their destination

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.740
and then charging for 24 hours.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:40.740
Wait a minute!

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.880
Why are we even bothering to move a whole
space station around?!

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:48.540
By calling it a “station,” you’re saying
you intend to keep it “stationary.”

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:53.620
Besides, a moon-sized steel ball is going
to be difficult to hide for 24 hours.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.120
Whose idea was this, anyway?!

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.180
Wouldn’t it just be easier to have the weapon
separate from the station?

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:02.420
The weapon detaches from the Death Star goes
wherever you need it to go,

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:07.440
grabs onto a nice big asteroid, takes 24-hours
to turn its mass into usable energy,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.580
and then fires.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:12.180
The weapon can be small, so it’s easier to hide
and it’s a lot more mobile.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.199
At which point, why do you even need the station?

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:17.420
It’s a huge waste of time and money if all
you’re trying to do is build a weapon.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.440
So, do you think the Death Star was a good
idea?

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:21.320
I’d like to hear your thoughts in the comments.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.040
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.380
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

